course: dictionary
lesson: 6267
title: Monarchy: rule by a king or queen
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A monarchy is a country ruled by a king, queen or emperor, and the position is usually passed on in the family. It is a noun, so you can say a monarchy, or the monarchy. Look at the word parts: monarch means ruler, and the ending -y means a system or a state of being ruled that way.
explain: «The French Revolution began in 1789, ended the monarchy in 1792 and spread new ideas about rights and citizens across Europe.» Ended the monarchy shows that the country stopped being ruled by a king or queen. Check by swapping in your meaning: "ended rule by a king or queen" still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: «The King is head of state in a constitutional monarchy: he does not run the country or take sides.» Head of state means the official leader of the country. The word constitutional tells you the king's powers are limited by rules, so he is not allowed to do whatever he likes. The monarchy can also mean the royal family itself, as in «The King and the royal family stay neutral.»
fact: A monarchy is a country ruled by a king, queen or emperor.
fact: The UK is a constitutional monarchy: the King is head of state, but Parliament makes the laws.
fact: The Prime Minister leads the government, not the King.
fact: Canada, Australia and New Zealand share the same monarch as the UK.
fact: The USA and Ireland are republics, with an elected president instead of a king or queen.
exercise: «The French Revolution ended the monarchy in 1792.» What does monarchy mean here? | A group of people who vote for a president | *A country ruled by a king or queen | A kind of boat used on rivers | A large bank of coins
exercise: In a constitutional monarchy like the UK, who leads the government? | The president, elected by the people | The King alone | The royal family, all together | *The Prime Minister
exercise: Which country has an elected president instead of a king or queen? | *The USA | Australia | Canada | New Zealand
test: «The King is head of state in a constitutional monarchy: he does not run the country.» What does constitutional tell you about the king's power? | *It is limited by rules | It is only for the royal family | It is unlimited, so he can do anything | It means he is elected every four years
test: The word monarchy can mean the royal family as well as the system. Which sentence uses it that way? | The sun rose early over the town | The boat sailed slowly across the lake | *The King and the royal family stay neutral | Rain fell all day over the hills
exam: Why do some people support the monarchy while others want a republic? | Because the rules are the same in every country | *Because they hold different views about having a king or queen | Because every republic has a king as its president | Because a monarchy is a kind of sport
